providerResponses.text:
{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "A working Python script that reads a small expenses CSV with csv.reader, groups rows by category into totals, and skips/reports malformed rows with a clear message, demonstrated on a file you create and run yourself."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "ROADMAP",
          "outcome": "Build a small CSV expenses report in Python that groups by category, reports totals, and checks malformed rows.",
          "explanation": "You have never read a CSV in Python, so the useful next checkpoint is a first working end-to-end script rather than the full eventual project. The three requested capabilities (CSV reading, grouping totals, malformed-row handling) are small enough to be learned together inside one integrated build: reading the file, accumulating per-category totals, and validating each row are naturally interleaved steps of the same script. Explanation is given during the build, and the independent changed example is a second small CSV you write and run without step-by-step help, which is the evidence that the checkpoint works. Later engineering (larger files, argparse, tests, packaging, richer reports) stays strategic and unbooked until this checkpoint is independently demonstrated and you explicitly ask to expand.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 90,
            "likelyMinutes": 150,
            "highMinutes": 240
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_build",
              "title": "First working expenses report",
              "outcome": "A runnable script that reads an expenses CSV, prints per-category totals, and reports malformed rows, verified on a self-made second file.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 90,
                "likelyMinutes": 150,
                "highMinutes": 240
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "The script runs on a new small CSV you create, prints correct category totals, and clearly reports at least one malformed row without crashing."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the build: opening the file and iterating rows with csv.reader, including the header row."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the build: accumulating a per-category total in a dictionary and printing the sorted result."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Taught and used inside the build: detecting rows with the wrong number of fields or a non-numeric amount and reporting them instead of crashing."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_4",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The integrated deliverable of this checkpoint: the small expenses report script itself, run on your own data."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "build_report",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_build"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Build and verify a small CSV expenses report",
            "activityType": "Guided build with independent changed example",
            "purpose": "Learn CSV reading, category grouping and malformed-row handling by producing one working script, then prove it on a new file you make yourself.",
            "instructions": "Work in a single Python file, e.g. expenses.py, with a small expenses.csv you create containing columns date,category,amount and a few rows.\n\n1. Reading (scope_1): import csv, open the file with open('expenses.csv', newline='') and iterate csv.reader. Print each row first so you see the header and data rows as lists. Note that the header is just the first row and must be skipped before treating rows as data.\n\n2. Grouping totals (scope_2): create totals = {}. For each data row, take category = row[1] and amount = float(row[2]), then totals[category] = totals.get(category, 0) + amount. After the loop, print each category and its total, e.g. for category in sorted(totals): print(category, totals[category]). Run it and check the totals by hand against your CSV.\n\n3. Malformed rows (scope_3): before converting, check len(row) != 3 and report that row as malformed (print a message with the row and continue). Wrap the float conversion in try/except ValueError and report the row as malformed on failure instead of letting the program crash. Add one deliberately bad row to your CSV (e.g. a missing amount or text in the amount column) and confirm the script reports it and still prints totals for the good rows.\n\n4. Independent changed example (scope_4): without following the steps above again, create a second small CSV with different categories and at least one malformed row, run your script on it, and check that the printed totals match what you compute by hand and that the malformed row is reported. If something is wrong, fix the script and re-run until both checks pass.",
            "completionCriterion": "Your script runs on a new small CSV you created, prints per-category totals that match hand calculation, and reports at least one malformed row with a clear message while still printing totals for valid rows.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 90,
              "likelyMinutes": 150,
              "highMinutes": 240
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 150,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 60,
                "completionCriterion": "Script reads the CSV and prints per-category totals for a clean file, checked by hand."
              },
              {
                "afterMinutes": 110,
                "completionCriterion": "Script reports malformed rows without crashing and still prints totals for valid rows."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "You have basic Python syntax (variables, loops, dictionaries, functions) available; if not, the first part of the build will need to slow down, which the effort range allows for."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "Only the first working checkpoint is booked; larger files, command-line arguments, tests and packaging are deliberately left strategic so the small report can actually be finished and verified."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "How quickly csv.reader and dictionary accumulation click for you is unknown, so the effort range is wide; the split points let the work stop cleanly at a partial milestone if a day runs short."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the script passes the independent changed-example check; do not add extra features or a second full re-check of already verified rows."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The requested outcome is a small integrated script, so the plan books one build activity that teaches CSV reading, grouping and malformed-row handling together and verifies them on a new file you create. This keeps the checkpoint small and finishable within the 60-minute daily ceiling using the declared split points, while leaving later engineering, tooling and larger-project milestones as strategic, unbooked scope until this checkpoint is independently demonstrated and you ask to expand."
}

